1
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:17,830
This is the eighth and final video presentation for the case animal and plant biotechnology course.

2
00:00:17,830 --> 00:00:26,830
As you view this video, be sure to make notes of important information. Feel free to pause, repeat, and resume the video whenever necessary.

3
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:33,830
Moving research into production. Lesson 5.2 from lab to production.

4
00:00:33,830 --> 00:00:45,830
You completed project 5.2.2 regulatory roundup, during which you determine the influence of governmental regulatory agencies in biotechnology application safety.

5
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:54,830
This presentation provides information about the steps and processes involved in bringing a new biotechnology application to fruition.

6
00:00:54,830 --> 00:01:01,830
Moving a new discovery from research into production requires a great deal of time, testing, and trials.

7
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:07,830
There are many phases to test the reliability of the products and the possible effects it may have when produced.

8
00:01:07,830 --> 00:01:13,830
Critical stages include manufacturing, risk analysis, regulation, and testing.

9
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:22,940
Good manufacturing practices are a set of standards that are enforced by the FDA.

10
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:31,940
GMPs ensure that safe practices are used throughout the production process and that all materials are of high quality grade.

11
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:41,940
Workers must follow proper laboratory practices, facilities must be maintained, and formal processes for developing and testing must be followed.

12
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:46,940
All products must be demonstrated to be of consistent quality and form.

13
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:56,620
Risk analysis is used to determine how safe a new technology is and how it should be monitored.

14
00:01:56,620 --> 00:02:02,620
All risks are weighed, measured, and evaluated against the potential benefits of the technology.

15
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:12,540
Questions and considerations for a risk analysis differ based on the organism being modified and the product desired.

16
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:27,540
Considerations when conducting a risk analysis include resistance, negative characteristics, target species, impacts, health effects, non-target species impacts, and potential for transfer.

17
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:36,600
Potential threats or risks are categorized according to many factors.

18
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:47,600
Products with low levels of transferability and reversible effects require less monitoring than those with high levels of transferability and irreversible effects.

19
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:54,600
The index of risk takes into account the damage, exposure, and precedent.

20
00:02:54,600 --> 00:03:00,600
Damage is the level or intensity of the impact that the GMP could have on the system.

21
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:04,600
If the proposed adverse effect actually occurs.

22
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:14,600
Exposure related to the level that some component, for example soil, animal, native plant, is exposed to the damage.

23
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:20,600
The higher the exposure, the higher the possibility of an adverse effect occurring.

24
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:28,600
Presence event considers the previous occurrence of the adverse effect as a consequence of the event in question.

25
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:37,600
The index of significance takes into account the extent and reversibility and includes the location where the GMP will be cultivated.

26
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:45,600
The identification and evaluation of the potential adverse effects and the evaluation of the current environmental situation.

27
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:51,600
Extent reflects the extent of dispersal or the distribution of the damage.

28
00:03:51,600 --> 00:04:00,640
Reversibility is the ability of the system to return to the previous condition.

29
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:08,640
Regulatory agencies studied in the previous activity play big roles in the process involved in bringing new biotechnologies to the marketplace.

30
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:23,060
Products must undergo extensive testing in order to determine the reliability of the results and all potential effects, side effects, and other outcomes.

31
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:32,860
If lab testing has positive results, the product may move into limited field testing.

32
